The short help Click derives from a command's help text (shown in the command list of a group's --help) stops at an abbreviation inside the first sentence:

```python
>>> from click.utils import _make_default_short_help
>>> _make_default_short_help("Weigh apples vs. pears.", 30)
'Weigh apples vs.'                    # expected 'Weigh apples vs. pears.'
```

Expected behaviour of click.utils._make_default_short_help(help, max_length):
- only the first paragraph counts; newlines, tabs and runs of spaces collapse to one space; a leading "\b" (no-rewrap marker) is ignored;
- a word ending in "." ends the first sentence when it is the last word, or when the next word does not start with a lowercase letter (so "Pick a fruit. 3 remain." stops after "fruit."); a following lowercase word continues the sentence ("vs. pears", "9. aaaa");
- the text up to that sentence end (or all of it) is returned as is when it fits in max_length;
- otherwise it is shortened at a word boundary with "..." so that the result, "..." included, fits max_length: "Weigh apples vs. pears and plums." at 20 gives "Weigh apples vs....";
- a word is never split, hyphenated words included: "well-behaved." at 8 gives "...", "supercalifragilistic" at 10 gives "...";
- when max_length is too small even for "..." (3 or less, zero or negative) and the text does not fit, the result is "..." ("ab cd" at 2 or -1 gives "..."), while a text that fits is returned whole ("ab" at 2 gives "ab").

